\ A `once` word's cell survives the freeze into a runtime-image AOT binary and behaves the way
\ the interpreter's does: the body runs on the first read, and every later read returns what it
\ published rather than running again.
\
\ The run counter is what makes a second cell visible; the printed value alone would look the
\ same either way.
\
\ Three shapes are exercised, because they reach the cell by different routes: a word whose body
\ calls another word, a word read from two separate call sites, and a body that throws and so
\ leaves the cell cold for the next call to retry.

\ Counts body entries. A binding rather than a `once` word, so it is built before anything reads
\ it and the counter itself never depends on the mechanism under test.
runs: ( -- vec ) V{ } ;

(note-run): ( -- ) [ runs 1 #push! drop ] ;

answer: ( -- fixnum ) once [ (note-run) 6 7 * ] ;

\ Two call sites, so a second cell would show up as a second run.
read-once: ( -- fixnum ) [ answer ] ;
read-twice: ( -- fixnum ) [ answer answer + ] ;

runs #len .   \ 0  -- nothing runs until the word is referenced
read-once .   \ 42 -- the first read computes
runs #len .   \ 1
read-twice .  \ 84 -- both reads reuse
runs #len .   \ 1  -- still one body entry across three reads from two call sites
\ A throwing body leaves the cell cold, so the next call runs it again. `attempts` crosses the
\ freeze as a binding and the body reads it, which also proves a cell body reaches module state.
attempts: ( -- vec ) V{ } ;

flaky: ( -- fixnum ) once [
  attempts 1 #push!
  #len 3 < [ f "not ready" EUserThrown make-error throw ] when
  99
] ;

[ flaky ] [ drop "threw" print-line ] recover
[ flaky ] [ drop "threw" print-line ] recover
flaky .          \ 99 -- the third attempt succeeds
flaky .          \ 99 -- and is reused
attempts #len .  \ 3  -- two failures left the cell cold, the third published
